---
// /blog/pure-veg-no-egg/ — added 2026-08-31 on the owner's ask: people search
// for "pure veg" and "eggless" before they search for a dish, and nothing on
// the site answered that question head-on.
//
// The claim is strong, so it has to be exactly true and nothing more: no egg
// in any item, no non-veg ever cooked here, one kitchen with nothing to keep
// separate. That is all defensible. It deliberately does NOT claim Jain --
// onion and garlic are in almost everything we make, and root vegetables at
// that. Saying "Jain" would send someone the wrong food.
//
// Every fact comes from site.config.json / menu.json via lib/data (PRD §6).
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
import BlogPostLayout from '../../components/BlogPostLayout.astro';
import { site, categories } from '../../lib/data';
import { LINKS, abs, blogPostingJsonLd, breadcrumbJsonLd } from '../../lib/seo';

const headline = 'Is It Really Pure Veg? No Egg, No Shared Kitchen';
const description =
  'Every item at The Oven Vibe is 100% pure vegetarian and completely eggless — no egg in the base, the sauce or the mayo, and no non-veg has ever been cooked in this kitchen.';
const image = {
  url: abs('/static/images/og/blog_5.jpg'),
  width: 1200,
  height: 630,
  alt: 'Pure veg eggless pizza in Sundargarh',
};
const path = '/blog/pure-veg-no-egg/';
const itemCount = categories.reduce((n, c) => n + c.items.length, 0);
---

<Layout
  title={headline}
  description={description}
  ogType="article"
  image={image}
  jsonLd={[
    blogPostingJsonLd({
      headline,
      description,
      path,
      image: image.url,
      datePublished: '2026-08-31',
    }),
    breadcrumbJsonLd([
      { name: 'Blog', path: '/blog/' },
      { name: 'Pure Veg, No Egg', path },
    ]),
  ]}
>
  <BlogPostLayout
    line1="PURE VEG,"
    lineGradient="NO EGG."
    subtitle="The question people actually ask before they order — answered properly."
  >
    <p class="subtitle" style="font-weight: 600; color: #555; margin-top: -10px">
      Sach mein pure veg hai na?
    </p>

    <picture>
      <source type="image/avif" srcset="/static/images/blog_images/blog_5.avif" />
      <source type="image/webp" srcset="/static/images/blog_images/blog_5.webp" />
      <img
        src="/static/images/blog_images/blog_5.webp"
        alt="Pure veg eggless pizza in Sundargarh"
        class="blog-img"
        loading="lazy"
        width="1024"
        height="1024"
        style="background: #eee"
      />
    </picture>

    <p>
      It is the first thing a lot of people in Sundargarh ask, and usually the last thing a menu
      answers. So here it is plainly: <strong>every single item we make is 100% pure vegetarian,
      and every single item is eggless.</strong> All {itemCount} of them. There is no asterisk.
    </p>

    <h2>What "eggless" means here</h2>
    <p>
      Egg hides in more places than people expect, which is why "veg" on its own is not an answer.
      Three of them matter on a pizza menu:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>The base.</strong> Ours has no egg in it. Nothing is brushed with egg wash.</li>
      <li>
        <strong>The mayo and the dips.</strong> Ordinary mayonnaise is made with egg. Ours is
        eggless — that is the only kind that comes into this kitchen.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>The sauces.</strong> No egg in the pizza sauce, the white sauce or the chutney.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Not "veg-friendly" — actually a veg-only kitchen</h2>
    <p>
      Plenty of places serve a veg menu out of a kitchen that also cooks meat. Same pans, same oven,
      same hands. For a lot of families that is the whole problem, and it is not something a label
      on the menu fixes.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Nothing non-veg has ever been cooked here.</strong> Not on a different shelf, not on a
      different day, not for a special order. There is one kitchen and one set of pans, so there is
      nothing to keep separate and nothing to get wrong on a busy night.
    </p>

    <div class="highlight-box">
      <h2>The short version</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>100% pure vegetarian — every item, always</li>
        <li>Completely <strong>eggless</strong> — base, mayo, dips and sauces</li>
        <li>No non-veg has ever been cooked in this kitchen</li>
        <li>Nothing shared, so nothing to cross-contaminate</li>
      </ul>
      <p style="margin-top: 10px; font-weight: 700; color: #ad210a">
        Order at any hour without asking the question first.
      </p>
    </div>

    <h2>One thing we will not claim</h2>
    <p>
      We are not a Jain kitchen. Onion and garlic go into most of what we cook, and both are root
      vegetables. We would rather say that here than have someone order in good faith and get food
      they cannot eat. If you avoid onion and garlic, call us before ordering and we will tell you
      honestly what is possible.
    </p>

    <h2>Why this is on the menu card too</h2>
    <p>
      The green mark next to every item is not decoration — it is the same fact, in the place you
      are looking when you decide. Have a look at the <a href="/menu/">full menu</a>: the pizzas,
      the grilled sandwiches, the pastas, the Maggi and the sides are all in the same one category.
    </p>

    <h2>The last bite</h2>
    <p>
      A pure veg kitchen should not be a thing you have to investigate. Ours is the whole business,
      not a section of it — so order the paneer pizza at 1 AM and think about nothing else.
    </p>

    <a href={`tel:${site.business.phone}`} class="cta">
      Call the Kitchen: {site.business.phone_display}
    </a>
    <p>
      Prefer to type? <a href={LINKS.whatsapp} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"
        >Order on WhatsApp</a
      >.
    </p>
  </BlogPostLayout>
</Layout>
